skyscrapers may define modern urban  skylines but cities existed for  thousands of years before steel frame  structures vaulted mankind upward cities  exist to bring people together and they  have always been shaped by the  transportation and construction  technologies of their errors the  pre-modern city was built for  pedestrians who walked through often  filthy streets and climbed dark  stairwells to fifth-floor Garrett's we  have individual structures like the  Colosseum in the Tower of London that  survived from ancient cities but we have  fewer examples of whole city escapes  that remain as legacies of the past  Pompeii is one such example frozen in  time by the eruption of Vesuvius in 79  ad he provides us with a remarkable  glimpse of urban life in the age of  Imperial Rome the city had once been an  independent ally of Rome but it became  permanently part of the Roman world  after it was occupied by Lucius  Cornelius sulla the Romans built  infrastructure such as spectacular  aqueducts the multitude of fountains  that dot Pompeii testified to the  importance of imported water for daily  urban life the local economy was of  course largely agricultural the rich  volcanic soil nearby produced abundant  grain and good wine but it also had  something of a tourist economy as  wealthy Romans built villas in Pompeii  the city is highly compact and  well-organized with a simple grid  organizing part of the town the  structures are typically short houses  usually have one or two storeys although  sometimes they sit atop ruined older  structures homes will often have an  interior atrium a typical Roman feature  because the Mediterranean climate makes  outdoor living ideal archeologists have  also identified about 100 benches  outside the structures which make it  possible to comfortably wait for  entrance into the home of a powerful  patron rooms are typically quite dark  glass windows seem to have been used by  Romans only after Pompey's destruction  large openings would have exposed homes  both to the elements and to thievery  exquisite paintings both inside and out  bring beauty into these otherwise stock  structures Romans also saw little value  in segregating work in home life  workshops were often inserted into the  ground floor of dwellings craftsmen  would sleep above  enjoy the shortest possible commutes  down a flight of stairs Bruges is a  slightly more modern preserved city it  became frozen in time not by a volcano  but because the silting of the Sven  River in the late 15th century radically  reduced its economic importance  consequently this medieval mega city  became brutally more a dead town  revealing its past whereas Pompeii had  perhaps 11,000 inhabitants at its height  by 1400 Bruges had a population well  over a hundred thousand Bruges as vast  wealth was built on trade in cloth which  had originally been woven from the hairs  of the shaggy sheep who lived nearby  like Venice Bruges is a city of canals  in evil times it was easier to move  goods on water than on land old Bruges  is compact like Pompeii built for  walking and barges homes are taller  often reaching three or four storeys  reflecting abundant demand for the  limited space within the city's walls  brood homes like atria but compensated  with glass windows this older city  required more protection from the  elements including snow and its distinct  Gables are both architectural adornment  and a sensible way to avoid the  accumulation of snowfall as the city  grew richer its wealthier inhabitants  built up and out the grew house which  housed aristocrats is opulent with four  visible rows of windows the house of the  Burroughs family also rises to four  storeys that family began as innkeepers  whose centrally located structure became  a meeting place for finance seers  trading both knowledge and investments  their name which became bourse  now labels stock exchanges throughout  the world four or five stories was  typically the upper limit on pre 