ell  aesthetic grandeur these structures  reflect the wealth of industrializing  England that was poured into growing  consumer cities as London grew richer  and larger the demand for space  propelled buildings upward the Midland  Grand Hotel built at the st. Pancras  station soars to 270 feet elevators made  height easier but the Midland Grande  still has a spectacular central  staircase the structures vast medieval  walls remind us of the difficulty of  building up before steel frames today we  are charmed by pre-modern urban spaces  they are so fundamentally different than  our elevator oriented skyscrapers and  car-based suburbs  yet these pre-modern spaces were still  about enabling mankind to work together  in urban agglomerations older urban  spaces provide a window into the lives  of the past that are both so similar and  so different from our lives today  you
 

